# .clauderc_memory — persistent context, portable across machines/failures
# This is the durable memory file. Unlike Claude Code's built-in per-machine
# memory (~/.claude/projects/.../memory/), THIS file is meant to be committed
# and pushed, so a full computer death doesn't lose it. Read this at the start
# of every session, after .clauderc. Update it as things change — prune stale
# entries rather than letting them silently rot.
#
# Format: dated entries, most recent state first per topic. Keep entries
# honest about what's confirmed vs. hypothesis vs. failed.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
HOW TO USE THIS FILE (meta)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

- This file holds CROSS-CUTTING material only — things with no single home
  directory (standing feedback, git/credential hygiene, the repo map itself).
  Anything scoped to one repo lives in .clauderc_context_1 under that repo's
  own heading instead (2026-07-07 restructure — directory-scoped content
  used to live here, migrated out to keep this file from duplicating
  context_1 and to keep each directory's context pure/self-contained).
- Prefer `tree` over brute-force `find` for exploration (Cody's correction,
  2026-07-07) — see .clauderc for the pattern.
- Repo paths/URLs live in .clauderc, not here.
- Canonical equations/notation live in .clauderc_canonical_maths, not here —
  this file should describe STATE and DECISIONS, not re-derive the maths.
- When a hypothesis is tested and fails, record the failure and WHY, not just
  "tried X, didn't work" — the diagnosis is what prevents repeating it.

